 "Mina and Sora have the same number of songs in a playlist. A total of $58%$ of Mina's songs are from the 2000s, while $\\frac{13}{20}$ of Sora's songs are from the 2000s. Select the options that make the statements below true.\n\n$\\,$\nA total of \n{{field-1}}\nof Sora's songs are from the 2000s. \n\n$\\,$\nTherefore, she has \n{{field-2}}\nsongs from the 2000s compared to Mina." :question/worked-solution "We need to compare $\\frac{13}{20}$ with $58%.$ We can do this by writing $\\frac{13}{20}$ as a percent.\n\nFirst, we write $\\frac{13}{20}$ as an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{13}{20}=\\frac{13\\times 5}{20\\times 5}=\\frac{65}{100}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\frac{65}{100}=65%\n$$\n\nSince $65%>58%,$ we have the following:\n\n- A total of $65%$ of Sora's songs are from the 2000s.\n\n- Therefore, she has $\\text{more}$ songs from the 2000s compared to Mina."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424819 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133740 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "70e7585e-7380-5dbc-9272-b358f085429c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Cube of a Fraction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a08369b-6b65-52e8-b39a-66fbd050d4f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424637} {:answer/id #uuid "453a9f35-6410-5144-9893-cb25c67ef3ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424669} {:answer/id #uuid "ae8805cc-ee6d-5bda-b7f8-dd5d735a4708" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424734} {:answer/id #uuid "ede1a3ee-2a40-5f2a-8f80-4edc4f6cfb7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8}{27}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424765} {:answer/id #uuid "f0f1f776-fa45-5415-a194-c5b09b2b9136" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{27}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424767}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ede1a3ee-2a40-5f2a-8f80-4edc4f6cfb7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8}{27}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424765} :answer-field/id #uuid "3743655a-aaa2-5711-89b6-70f7921c6f1e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424784}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4dd6b555-5ecc-595c-aed6-3fa1e6dff4c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-41363" :question/problem "${(-\\frac{2}{3})}^{3}=$" :question/worked-solution "To work out ${(-\\frac{2}{3})}^{3},$ we multiply $-\\frac{2}{3}$ by itself and then multiply the result by $-\\frac{2}{3}$ again:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(-\\frac{2}{3})}^{3} & =(-\\frac{2}{3})\\cdot (-\\frac{2}{3})\\cdot (-\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2}{3\\cdot 3\\cdot 3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{27}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424816 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064231 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c76b2b1b-7536-5b3c-a4c4-6f5e5b72e6ec" :knowledge-point/title "Dividing a Number by Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05897e61-41a9-514e-b9be-e42a73520d18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424633} {:answer/id #uuid "2a734bd6-c5da-5171-a2e1-5a7554594aeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424656} {:answer/id #uuid "636c0f10-fd0f-5aa1-b437-63bb6b77b56c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424687} {:answer/id #uuid "e9cb110f-2081-5793-9d42-322cb1d7ddb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424761} {:answer/id #uuid "ea83ba1b-49b7-5c2b-ae70-9177785e563b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424762}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "636c0f10-fd0f-5aa1-b437-63bb6b77b56c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424687} :answer-field/id #uuid "da79e43d-9229-50b1-8f6e-db23fb52b3ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424803}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3913baef-cd96-5f27-bb8b-fbabac946e82" :question/math-academy-id "q-43156" :question/problem "Which of the following is undefined?\n\nI. $0\\cdot 0$\n\nII. $\\frac{2}{0}$\n\nIII. $\\frac{0}{-1}$" :question/worked-solution "We consider each point individually:\n\n- Multiplying any number by zero always gives zero. Therefore, $0\\cdot 0=0.$\n\n- We cannot divide any number by zero. Therefore, $\\frac{2}{0}=2÷0$ is undefined.\n\n- Zero divided by any number (other than zero) equals zero. Therefore, $\\frac{0}{-1}=0÷(-1)=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424815 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054809 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7fcec0d8-ca03-5446-a42f-65d0ccddd584" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Quadrant of a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35f8d103-6f29-5ff2-9780-13b3c5bde7b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424664} {:answer/id #uuid "6b578253-f653-5277-90e0-6ff1856c7169" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424694} {:answer/id #uuid "71100155-802b-5a00-9ce5-6e22f9061530" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424695} {:answer/id #uuid "99ce13dd-f4f2-5dc8-9b01-79f8a15345b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424721} {:answer/id #uuid "f1570302-604e-51de-9dc5-a369c259058c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424768}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1570302-604e-51de-9dc5-a369c259058c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424768} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5011262-91bb-52ce-8cce-e7c54b0fa5f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424800}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2919c042-14cc-564a-b488-e11edad346eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-43595" :question/problem "Which of the following points lies  in the fourth quadrant?" :question/worked-solution "- The point $(2,-2)$ is below and to the right of the origin, so it is in the fourth quadrant $✓$\n\n- The point $(1,1)$ is above and to the right of the origin, so it is in the first quadrant $\\times $\n\n- The point $(2,3)$ is above and to the right of the origin, so it is in the first quadrant $\\times $\n\n- The point $(-1,-1)$ is below and to the left of the origin, so it is in the third quadrant $\\times $\n\n- The point $(-2,2)$ is above and to the left of the origin, so it is in the second quadrant $\\times $"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424821 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192729 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "241b7ea0-ba03-538f-a4f0-18739424e576" :knowledge-point/title "Perpendicular Lines"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "028c4ad2-d8c1-5a75-b544-74cc2cb5505c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$q⊥m$ and $r⊥n$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424628} {:answer/id #uuid "1643072f-0fdf-58de-b9a2-4eda8574e670" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$m⊥n$ and $r⊥s$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424647} {:answer/id #uuid "59245362-2696-5cfc-96ee-c61105c3e1d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$r⊥s$ and $q⊥m$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424681} {:answer/id #uuid "c0c1dfd7-2d24-5767-a5fd-015ebd557565" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$q⊥r$ and $q⊥s$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424741} {:answer/id #uuid "e40adac0-342c-5087-99d1-3c37023e0c63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$m⊥n$ and $q⊥n$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424756}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c0c1dfd7-2d24-5767-a5fd-015ebd557565" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$q⊥r$ and $q⊥s$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424741} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5d95232-4cd0-5474-a978-54ab30c5c42d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424802}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6d3b19cb-cf63-59ba-9591-d8e9da347a6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-52571" :question/problem "![](images/f1/f1c2ae86cd726df7a493351ad0cdb29fdb9767db75c25b432c1832f97865e1dc.png)\n\nFrom the diagram above, which of the following statements is true?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $a$ and $b$ are two lines. Then,\n\n- $a∥b$ means $a$ and $b$ are parallel, and\n\n- $a⊥b$ means $a$ and $b$ are perpendicular.\n\nIn the given diagram, there are two pairs of perpendicular lines:\n\n- $q$ is perpendicular to $r,$ which we write as $q⊥r$\n\n- $q$ is perpendicular to $s,$ which we write as $q⊥s.$\n\n![](images/06/063ecc105da75d9ba1b3c242476f1353dec82ca9ec13df48f4f480f5f490cb94.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct statement is $q⊥r$ and $q⊥s.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424810 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138400 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a49001aa-bf42-5005-9bf0-940caab49b8f" :knowledge-point/title "Writing Larger Exponents as Repeated Multiplication"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23948b42-b762-51e7-9468-ecef08521c35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "53,453" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424653} {:answer/id #uuid "5247dc82-e291-5e70-a5e1-a18611a34b47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "53\\times 53\\times 53\\times 53\\times 53" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424675} {:answer/id #uuid "7be5874e-a678-5f3a-8bc9-5fcf4ea6fa99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "534" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424702} {:answer/id #uuid "ccb628e0-3c1f-563b-bab3-4db236781bff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "53+53+53+53" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424747} {:answer/id #uuid "fb103597-39b6-5e4b-908b-9ef13f036e2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "53\\times 53\\times 53\\times 53" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424774}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb103597-39b6-5e4b-908b-9ef13f036e2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "53\\times 53\\times 53\\times 53" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424774} :answer-field/id #uuid "774dcce1-bb39-5483-867c-6e3177bc6b84" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424790}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0cbd63dc-7d38-513f-8022-cfd6f02074bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-55068" :question/problem "${53}^{4}=$" :question/worked-solution "Here, ${53}^{4}$ is in exponent form. The base is $53$ and the exponent is $4.$ \n\nThis means that $53$ is multiplied by itself $4$ times:\n\n$$\n{{53\\times 53\\times 53\\times 53}_{⏟}}_{4\\text{ times}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{53}^{4}=53\\times 53\\times 53\\times 53.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424818 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138577 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2c87e441-a534-5f6b-82e7-34e2ef8b9639" :knowledge-point/title "Subtracting Two Mixed Numbers Using the Least Common Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "044ddbb7-3b06-5e42-b282-688505f9b850" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,\\frac{5}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424630} {:answer/id #uuid "688087cd-e189-54aa-a799-aa440187e707" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,\\frac{1}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424693} {:answer/id #uuid "8daf47aa-95a4-5c58-bb83-ed057ccfe693" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424715} {:answer/id #uuid "e746a26b-089a-589b-8349-6b4c403a5d83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424759} {:answer/id #uuid "ea9638ad-3f35-5504-8114-b15ada1afa61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424763}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "688087cd-e189-54aa-a799-aa440187e707" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,\\frac{1}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424693} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9e0d1f0-a07e-50d3-bdd0-922f6849654a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424801}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4123159f-01d7-597f-9386-b9f6aae3c918" :question/math-academy-id "q-57929" :question/problem "$6\\,\\frac{7}{8}-1\\,\\frac{5}{6}=$" :question/worked-solution "To subtract two mixed numbers, we subtract whole numbers from whole numbers and fractions from fractions. \n\nSubtracting the whole numbers, we get $6-1=5.$\n\nNow we subtract the fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{8}-\\frac{5}{6}\n$$\n\nTo subtract the fractions, we need to find the least common multiple of the denominators ($8$ and $6$).\n\n- Multiples of 8: $\\,8,16,24,…$\n\n- Multiples of 6: $\\,6,12,18,24,…$\n\nTherefore, the least common denominator of $8$ and $6$ is $24.$\n\n- To put $\\frac{7}{8}$ over a denominator of $24,$ we multiply the numerator and denominator by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{8}=\\frac{7\\times 3}{8\\times 3}=\\frac{21}{24}\n$$\n\n- To put $\\frac{5}{6}$ over a denominator of $24,$ we multiply the numerator and denominator by $4$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{6}=\\frac{5\\times 4}{6\\times 4}=\\frac{20}{24}\n$$\n\nWe can now subtract the fractions. We keep the denominator the same, and we subtract the numerators:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{8}-\\frac{5}{6}=\\frac{21}{24}-\\frac{20}{24}=\\frac{1}{24}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n6\\,\\frac{7}{8}-1\\,\\frac{5}{6}=5\\,\\frac{1}{24}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424828 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073737 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9edb7c90-39fa-5396-b00e-04a0627d0581" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting Ratios"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c461ec9-bd83-56be-8a0b-16ab7c57af50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The bakery has exactly $14$ cakes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424659} {:answer/id #uuid "725845e5-d2a9-518e-a655-e1fadde2ac77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Out of every $5$ cakes in the bakery, $9$ of them are vanilla flavored cakes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424696} {:answer/id #uuid "79ea1599-2d15-5e66-913b-d62e805fbe0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The bakery has exactly $5$ vanilla flavored cakes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424700} {:answer/id #uuid "b3cff89c-df79-56ef-9fdb-17f38fe36528" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The bakery has exactly $9$ cakes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424735} {:answer/id #uuid "d1373d2c-5b68-5eec-8a50-708f71cf6136" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Out of every $9$ cakes in the bakery, $5$ of them are vanilla flavored cakes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424749}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d1373d2c-5b68-5eec-8a50-708f71cf6136" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Out of every $9$ cakes in the bakery, $5$ of them are vanilla flavored cakes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424749} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3caaf88-bd90-5d96-ae11-c1828ec82fce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424806}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c904b7d0-fa86-585f-90ce-acc0a098264d" :question/math-academy-id "q-60095" :question/problem "The ratio of vanilla flavored cakes to total cakes in a bakery is $5:9.$ What is the meaning of this ratio?" :question/worked-solution "If the ratio of vanilla flavored cakes to total cakes is $5:9,$ it means that there are $5$ vanilla flavored cakes for every $9$ cakes in total.\n\nAnother way of writing this is to say that \"out of every $9$ cakes in the bakery, $5$ of them are vanilla flavored cakes.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424835 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184147 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b88656f-353a-5d5a-88f7-c85a5c9eec3e" :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying Mixed Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19910fe0-21fa-590d-9e14-16d87e633948" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3\\,\\frac{5}{6}$ pages" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424648} {:answer/id #uuid "350e5a83-72fd-5449-869c-2ca5889ba8d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4\\,\\frac{1}{4}$ pages" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424662} {:answer/id #uuid "809a3adb-123f-5225-824e-c7b50246211a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5\\,\\frac{5}{6}$ pages" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424705} {:answer/id #uuid "c3c979bc-7021-5ad0-82bf-65684b6e3867" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4\\,\\frac{1}{6}$ pages" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424743} {:answer/id #uuid "e2b4f9d6-31c4-5850-8770-e44017316ac6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3\\,\\frac{3}{4}$ pages" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424753}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3c979bc-7021-5ad0-82bf-65684b6e3867" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4\\,\\frac{1}{6}$ pages" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424743} :answer-field/id #uuid "245282af-0a8d-5db9-8f51-9e1f9cb3d8fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424780}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f3718652-c7d4-58dc-86d1-2efd82a282a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-60613" :question/problem "Liza can read $1\\,\\frac{1}{4}$ pages of a book in a minute. How many pages would she read if she read for $3\\,\\frac{1}{3}$ minutes?" :question/worked-solution "To determine the number of pages Liza would have read in $3\\,\\frac{1}{3}$ minutes, we need to calculate $1\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\times 3\\,\\frac{1}{3}.$     \n\nFirst, we write  $1\\,\\frac{1}{4}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{(1\\times 4)+1}{4}=\\frac{5}{4}\n$$\n\nWe also write $3\\,\\frac{1}{3}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n3\\,\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{(3\\times 3)+1}{3}=\\frac{10}{3}\n$$\n\nSo now, we need to solve the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{4}\\times \\frac{10}{3}\n$$\n\nNotice that the first fraction's denominator ($4$) and the second fraction's numerator ($10$) have a common factor of $2.$ \n\nTherefore, we can simplify our problem by swapping the denominators first and then multiplying the resulting fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{4}\\times \\frac{10}{3} & =\\frac{5}{3}\\times \\frac{10}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\times \\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\times 5}{3\\times 2} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{25}{6}=4\\,\\text{R}\\,1=4\\,\\frac{1}{6}\n$$\n\nSo, Liza would have read $4\\,\\frac{1}{6}$ pages."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424807 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073893 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "477ee69a-937b-54cb-a2e7-4dc6f023f0bd" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting Percentages Greater Than 100% Using Other Models"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d03fd8a-2076-5486-b090-2107ac2b168e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3d/3d4e262561c33c1be8962c52b6e154d5f1f6558abfaf85135729765d411f57fc.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424640} {:answer/id #uuid "8376136f-b93e-50fe-b245-723cee5ebb23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b9/b901819e40d67559b5b8f6d137ab5595531801f5529f778be34c768b2dc40c3f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424710} {:answer/id #uuid "9dae7cb2-87d3-5ea1-a674-27dec871cabd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/6e/6e272cd71993ba8a344a52c7091f9bfc48e8873075d4e213adf63d0a7001bd6e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424723} {:answer/id #uuid "a5952940-6cca-53b8-9400-ded8211aa685" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/58/585fc1e5c1d4a4fb65d86f1691aa04205a652dd74c5c774806ad00c19f0b792c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424729} {:answer/id #uuid "f45fd230-ba7d-5a34-8e2c-05fca9ab91c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/90/904fbb7dc1eb30d4353c1234b9ef7039813bcfcdaef7e29d98ebdd0d054796d0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424770}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8376136f-b93e-50fe-b245-723cee5ebb23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b9/b901819e40d67559b5b8f6d137ab5595531801f5529f778be34c768b2dc40c3f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424710} :answer-field/id #uuid "11e659f3-5035-5397-8d32-e6e80649c6f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424779}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "00b9b682-db5e-578e-b943-e60736c094da" :question/math-academy-id "q-61586" :question/problem "If a circle represents a whole, which model shows $210%?$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall that $210%$ is the same as $\\frac{210}{100}.$\n\nWe now need to simplify this fraction. Dividing the numerator and denominator by $10,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{210}{100}=\\frac{210÷10}{100÷10}=\\frac{21}{10}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we write $\\frac{21}{10}$ as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{21}{10}=2\\,\\text{R}\\,1=2\\,\\frac{1}{10}\n$$\n\nThe model that shows $2\\,\\frac{1}{10}$ is:\n\n![](images/b9/b901819e40d67559b5b8f6d137ab5595531801f5529f778be34c768b2dc40c3f.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424813 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142761 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "03ba798b-dc64-5b63-86e6-1973b38f11bd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least Common Multiple of Three Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1da3f2c2-725f-5e06-b04a-3763010c9c6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424650} {:answer/id #uuid "626acdf3-4daa-58e1-9173-3bcdcd1012bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424686} {:answer/id #uuid "7acbbd28-5bb2-5eda-847c-32bedbb30634" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424701} {:answer/id #uuid "7c35c9df-5fe0-5f65-b063-53ece3fea7b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424703} {:answer/id #uuid "a3dd160a-d632-56c1-bf60-89162104c303" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424728}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1da3f2c2-725f-5e06-b04a-3763010c9c6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424650} :answer-field/id #uuid "4dc5e5bd-d306-5813-8c77-f367891a93b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424787}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "19af72ca-8e82-500b-a6d5-3bf3c4fb4f5d" :question/math-academy-id "q-63721" :question/problem "Determine the least common multiple of $4,$ $6$ and $8.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's list the multiples of each number. Then, we find the smallest number that appears in all three lists:\n\n- Multiples of $4$: $\\,4,8,12,16,20,24,28…$\n\n- Multiples of $6$: $\\,6,12,18,24,30…$\n\n- Multiples of $8$: $\\,8,16,24,32…$\n\nWe conclude that the least common multiple of  $4,$ $6$ and $8$ is $24.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424817 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054801 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8fc32821-14d0-5cbe-b8d9-4e7ff5cc937b" :knowledge-point/title "Translating Between Phrases and Algebraic Expressions Containing Subtraction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03cb47b4-1278-565e-9fc6-9ca296e32d59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424629} {:answer/id #uuid "3bcf3031-4224-5e16-aad5-be5ccf730fc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424668} {:answer/id #uuid "55a4c253-27fe-534e-9228-18adfd7921d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{b}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424679} {:answer/id #uuid "a21b1a6d-5cb1-5af8-89e7-aa6c782299e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424727} {:answer/id #uuid "f6db4c51-935c-5804-88e6-be5f7f658aa6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "b-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424772}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f6db4c51-935c-5804-88e6-be5f7f658aa6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "b-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424772} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c000ed0-a990-5060-9938-0ea03ff8141a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424792}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3e293dd9-81d5-5834-9d60-552159e9c226" :question/math-academy-id "q-67037" :question/problem "Translate the following phrase into an algebraic expression: \"$2$ less than a number.\"" :question/worked-solution "If we denote our number by $b$, then the phrase can be written as\n\n$$\nb-2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424808 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142775 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "91037b32-189a-5ba9-ab0a-117f6255c496" :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying a Fraction by a Whole Number Resulting in a Mixed Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11b32ef8-7079-5370-bf27-4720f45f1a06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424645} {:answer/id #uuid "1e0e505b-4b6e-5114-bf92-fa4a928bb1aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424651} {:answer/id #uuid "80e346b2-a78f-5925-92d6-d99fbbc0ea9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424706} {:answer/id #uuid "80e8c6b3-f9d1-5d0e-a9dc-3967d6d9597d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424707} {:answer/id #uuid "ef102bfd-60f2-5cc3-a0f2-c1920a9ada0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424766}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80e8c6b3-f9d1-5d0e-a9dc-3967d6d9597d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424707} :answer-field/id #uuid "37466fa8-bdd5-5cbb-be42-dfa817c1501e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424785}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "028c8481-ba77-5958-9c80-addca2355a16" :question/math-academy-id "q-70168" :question/problem "$\\frac{7}{10}\\times 6=$" :question/worked-solution "To multiply a fraction by a whole number, we multiply the fraction's numerator by the whole number and keep the denominator the same:\n\n$$\n\\frac{7\\times 6}{10}=\\frac{42}{10}\n$$\n\nWe then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\frac{42}{10}=\\frac{42÷2}{10÷2}=\\frac{21}{5}\n$$\n\nFinally, we then convert $\\frac{21}{5}$ to a mixed number:\n\n$$\n21÷5=4\\,\\text{R}1=4\\,\\frac{1}{5}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424827 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073712 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "15b6296b-11a1-53da-a243-aacc734e82cb" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Addition Problems Using Fraction Models"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "387b3a40-26eb-5334-89c6-53446c8732c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{26}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424666} {:answer/id #uuid "4af6b263-2e47-510a-bfbf-e39fff2b4c82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{29}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424672} {:answer/id #uuid "5add4d61-0540-554f-b083-db37ab4d04ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424682} {:answer/id #uuid "8fe6a6a3-7555-5f62-9765-7009e47d333d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{22}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424716} {:answer/id #uuid "bb5c6573-cb9f-5830-8333-8f3d1c0bc0e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{23}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424736}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bb5c6573-cb9f-5830-8333-8f3d1c0bc0e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{23}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424736} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a433299-3b2f-59ad-8cd3-e9a8ddbee98a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424795}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bee69b89-dd1d-5978-a866-55d032eb0cd5" :question/math-academy-id "q-71254" :question/problem "Use a visual fraction model to find the value of $3+\\frac{2}{7}$ as an improper fraction." :question/worked-solution "Writing $3+\\frac{2}{7}$ as a visual fraction model, we get the following:\n\n![](images/97/9797ccbc74bee1628f6c056415b831ee315d1e5d5b6c01496abdcc7b62537b2b.png)\n\nThe shape on the right is split into $7$ equal parts. We can split the first, second, and third shapes into $7$ equal parts as follows:\n\n![](images/3b/3b8c180326206b70caf96caef6d560ac57621a61a5bd3fc85d9e3bb1e7dfebf8.png)\n\nThere are $7+7+7+2=23$ shaded parts in total. Therefore, their sum is $\\frac{23}{7}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424809 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073723 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "79e310e4-b8a2-5327-b4e6-fd71b6783e3a" :knowledge-point/title "Dividing Whole Numbers by Unit Fractions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c95716f-cf83-52c8-ad2c-b3320e3a7bc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424639} {:answer/id #uuid "2a1de6ad-d794-5f0b-bb99-d84a2f50f2f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424655} {:answer/id #uuid "98d8b381-abed-5636-adc1-6d9124007ad7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424719} {:answer/id #uuid "c7d43e8c-2208-5f9d-acc5-836a88650ab3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424744} {:answer/id #uuid "caeb001f-cb2a-59fe-948d-d7bec3fd7f84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424746}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "98d8b381-abed-5636-adc1-6d9124007ad7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424719} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4ea0cbd-ce5a-544a-994e-2f0c60d8bf7d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424804}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "02ac172b-9db9-5580-848c-c810f3c4dd7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-92133" :question/problem "![](images/b1/b1c4d24b853c33ae4ba367761eb207eacf7cc46312077e3f21416cfc05f5ef3b.png)\n\nThe model above represents $2$ wholes. Use this model to calculate the value of $2÷\\frac{1}{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate $2÷\\frac{1}{3}$, we need to divide each of $2$ wholes into $3$ equal parts, as shown below.\n\n![](images/29/291ef5c96846fd1244b9be904da7d56db056befa0758b78c169f24b414d8b96e.png)\n\n \n\nWe get $2\\times 3=6$ pieces in total. \n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n2÷\\frac{1}{3}=6\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424823 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073879 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e191386-9391-5e3c-9f2f-f17d55f9b6ca" :knowledge-point/title "Completing a Statement Describing a Ratio as a Fraction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ee27ae2-8062-5b66-9e3a-c26d15da8b51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424642} {:answer/id #uuid "547a0586-4d5f-5b70-96b0-951a903887ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424678} {:answer/id #uuid "5b74ca5a-1cfa-5466-9d21-51a633975ff4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424683} {:answer/id #uuid "5cb3d36a-a378-5d2d-8d95-75f0d5fc2b37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424684} {:answer/id #uuid "67d96a93-9be5-5339-87e7-e13e341dba72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424692}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ee27ae2-8062-5b66-9e3a-c26d15da8b51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424642} :answer-field/id #uuid "71c4c23a-cd41-56c1-9bb1-541b365b7757" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424789}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7fb16d63-026a-5791-8719-a48e4a4e389e" :question/math-academy-id "q-92949" :question/problem "What is missing in the following sentence?\n\nIn a music school, the ratio of students in the violin class to students in the piano class is $32:4,$ so there are $\\boxed{}$ students in the violin class for each student in the piano class." :question/worked-solution "We can rewrite the ratio $32:4$ as the following fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{32}{4}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the fraction by dividing the numerator and denominator by $4:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{32}{4}=\\frac{32÷4}{4÷4}=\\frac{8}{1}=8\n$$\n\nThis means that there are $8$ students in the violin class for each student in the piano class."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424829 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152126 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f7063140-eebe-598c-b530-3c13509d359b" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Numbers as Rational"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65467c55-4012-565e-9f4c-86f2450043e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424689} {:answer/id #uuid "957bcbbb-38da-5714-9fd9-7d21a88cb06e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424718} {:answer/id #uuid "c9553ae8-cd77-5fb6-81e8-25583030b458" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424745} {:answer/id #uuid "cf4041ac-449a-56ee-bec7-41438a2640f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424748} {:answer/id #uuid "f6b336e0-5ad1-5621-824c-34d90d2ca6f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424771}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65467c55-4012-565e-9f4c-86f2450043e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424689} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b791c26-12be-57d7-a745-4c7665ba6d9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424796}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d38c8439-616e-58d4-9fa5-c5876ae08e79" :question/math-academy-id "q-99327" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $-\\frac{1}{9}$ is a natural number\n\nII. $-\\frac{1}{9}$ is an integer\n\nIII. $-\\frac{1}{9}$ is a rational number" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall our definitions.\n\n- The natural numbers consist of all positive whole numbers: $1,\\,2,\\,3,\\,4,\\,5,…$\n\n- The integers consist of all of the positive and negative whole numbers, and zero: $…,-3,\\,-2,\\,-1,\\,0,\\,1,\\,2,\\,3,…$\n\n- The rationals consist of all numbers that can be written as the ratio of two integers.\n\nNow, let's go through each number in turn.\n\n- $-\\frac{1}{9}$ is not a natural number.\n\n- $-\\frac{1}{9}$ is not an integer.\n\n- $-\\frac{1}{9}$ is a rational number because it can be written as $\\frac{(-1)}{9}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424824 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064364 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9cb97145-a93a-5df3-8dbe-6777d21601c8" :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying a Positive Number by a Negative Number With Decimals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a10fae3-fa17-5303-b596-fbef94ee75c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424638} {:answer/id #uuid "6735dbee-697e-5562-b172-b6a431f58469" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424690} {:answer/id #uuid "76fd92d2-9ca5-57c3-a239-a09331ef7c87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424699} {:answer/id #uuid "807843cb-43f6-5e98-aae1-651af93550fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424704} {:answer/id #uuid "e34a7f0c-4cb7-5fb3-84be-72c59447da6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424754}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76fd92d2-9ca5-57c3-a239-a09331ef7c87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424699} :answer-field/id #uuid "02c1e77d-1778-57eb-aa43-e7e946a8d6a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424777}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8d1edec4-291f-530b-ae4b-c5263dbe2c85" :question/math-academy-id "q-99463" :question/problem "$(-5)\\cdot (3.3)=$" :question/worked-solution "We have a product of a negative number $(-5)$ and a positive number $(3.3).$ So the product will be negative:\n\n$$\n(-5)\\cdot 3.3=-\\boxed{}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the number that goes in the box, we multiply $-5$ by $3.3$ without any signs: $5\\cdot 3.3=16.5.$ So, we get\n\n$$\n(-5)\\cdot 3.3=-16.5.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424825 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142840 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "db24977e-2538-5cd2-8c31-2e8f0e8ac134" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Reciprocal of a Mixed Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07ea3937-847f-5885-946b-426225372084" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424636} {:answer/id #uuid "2aa28cd2-74e3-5472-8aae-5815dea7bc58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424657} {:answer/id #uuid "2baa7521-888a-563a-b0f8-225f196a5a5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424658} {:answer/id #uuid "4a10bc1e-be32-55d6-a38c-66bd3233dc13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{23}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424671} {:answer/id #uuid "fbb6acdb-99dd-5803-914a-5bb743cbe4f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424775}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fbb6acdb-99dd-5803-914a-5bb743cbe4f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424775} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a9fdf29-bf95-5d1b-8fda-3e0a2d439219" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424786}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "905949ae-4813-5860-b334-182f5d44f611" :question/math-academy-id "q-99858" :question/problem "What is the reciprocal of $3\\,\\frac{2}{5}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the given mixed number to an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n3\\,\\frac{2}{5}=\\frac{(3\\times 5)+2}{5}=\\frac{17}{5}\n$$\n\nTo find the reciprocal of a fraction, we switch the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{17}{5}\\,\\to \\,\\frac{5}{17}\n$$\n\nSo the reciprocal of $\\frac{17}{5}$ is $\\frac{5}{17}.$"}]}
